In C++, real numbers are represented by the float, double, and long double data types. These data types differ from other data types in that they can represent fractional values as well as very large or very small values. 
Here is an example code that demonstrates the range of real numbers using the <iostream> and <limits> libraries:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>
using namespace std;
int main() {
  cout << "The range of float is from " << numeric_limits<float>::lowest() << " to " << numeric_limits<float>::max() << endl;
  cout << "The range of double is from " << numeric_limits<double>::lowest() << " to " << numeric_limits<double>::max() << endl;
  cout << "The range of long double is from " << numeric_limits<long double>::lowest() << " to " << numeric_limits<long double>::max() << endl;
  return 0;
}
```
This code uses the `numeric_limits` template class from the <limits> library to output the minimum and maximum values that can be represented by each real number data type.
In other programming languages such as Java and Python, real numbers are also represented by similar data types. However, the range of values that can be represented may differ depending on the language and the specific data type used.
Real number operations that can result in overflow or underflow include addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. For example, if two very large numbers are added together, the result may exceed the maximum value that can be represented by the data type and cause an overflow. Similarly, if a very small number is divided by another very small number, the result may be too small to be represented by the data type and cause an underflow.
To handle these cases in the code, you can check for overflow or underflow using conditional statements and handle the situation accordingly. For example, you can use the `isinf()` function to check if a value is infinity or the `isnan()` function to check if a value is NaN (not a number). You can also use exception handling to catch and handle these errors.

