How can you implement a binary search algorithm using C++ code? Can you provide an example code snippet using [array data] and [C++ code] formats to demonstrate your implementation? 
To get started, consider using an array of integers sorted in ascending order as input data. Then, implement a recursive function to perform the binary search algorithm. The function should take in the array, the lower and upper bounds of the search range, and the target value as parameters. 
Here's an example of a binary search implementation using C++ code and an input array:
[Array data]
[3, 5, 7, 10, 13, 15, 18, 20, 25]
[C++ code]
int binarySearch(int arr[], int low, int high, int target) {
  if (high >= low) {
    int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
    if (arr[mid] == target)
      return mid;
    if (arr[mid] > target)
      return binarySearch(arr, low, mid - 1, target);
    return binarySearch(arr, mid + 1, high, target);
  }
  return -1;
}
int main() {
  int arr[] = {3, 5, 7, 10, 13, 15, 18, 20, 25};
  int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
  int target = 18;
  int result = binarySearch(arr, 0, n - 1, target);
  (result == -1) ? cout << "Element is not present in array"
                 : cout << "Element is present at index " << result;
  return 0;
}
How can you modify this code to handle arrays with duplicate values and return the index of the first occurrence of the target value?

output:
To modify the code to handle arrays with duplicate values and return the index of the first occurrence of the target value, we need to make a small change in the binary search algorithm. Instead of returning the index as soon as we find the target element, we need to continue the search towards the left of the mid index until we find the first occurrence of the target element. Here's the modified code:
```
int binarySearch(int arr[], int low, int high, int target) {
  if (high >= low) {
    int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
    if (arr[mid] == target) {
      // check if this is the first occurrence of the target element
      if (mid == 0 || arr[mid - 1] != target)
        return mid;
      else
        return binarySearch(arr, low, mid - 1, target);
    }
    if (arr[mid] > target)
      return binarySearch(arr, low, mid - 1, target);
    return binarySearch(arr, mid + 1, high, target);
  }
  return -1;
}
int main() {
  int arr[] = {3, 5, 7, 10, 13, 15, 18, 18, 18, 20, 25};
  int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
  int target = 18;
  int result = binarySearch(arr, 0, n - 1, target);
  if (result == -1) {
    cout << "Element is not present in array";
  } else {
    // print the index of the first occurrence of the target element
    while (result > 0 && arr[result - 1] == target) {
      result--;
    }
    cout << "Element is present at index " << result;
  }
  return 0;
}
```
In this modified code, we check if the current mid index is the first occurrence of the target element. If it is, we return the mid index. Otherwise, we continue the search towards the left of the mid index until we find the first occurrence of the target element. Once we find the first occurrence, we print its index as the result.

