As for the detailed characteristics of each taxonomic group, here they are:
- Mammals: characterized by the presence of hair or fur, mammary glands, and the ability to regulate their body temperature internally.
  - Placental mammals: give birth to fully developed young.
  - Marsupials: give birth to underdeveloped young that continue to develop in a pouch.
  - Monotremes: lay eggs instead of giving birth.
- Birds: characterized by their feathers, beaks, and the ability to fly (in most cases).
  - Flightless birds: unable to fly.
  - Water birds: adapted to living in or near water.
  - Perching birds: able to perch on branches or other structures.
- Fish: characterized by their gills, scales, and fins.
  - Jawless fish: lack jaws and paired fins.
  - Cartilaginous fish: have a skeleton made of cartilage instead of bone.
  - Bony fish: have a skeleton made of bone.
- Reptiles: characterized by their scaly skin, ability to lay eggs on land, and inability to regulate their body temperature internally.
  - Turtles: have a protective shell.
  - Crocodilians: have a long snout and powerful jaws.
  - Lizards: have four legs and a long tail.
  - Snakes: have a long, flexible body and no legs.
- Insects: characterized by their three-part bodies, six legs, and often, wings.
  - Beetles: have hard, protective forewings.
  - Flies: have only one pair of wings.
  - Ants: live in large, organized colonies.
  - Bees: important pollinators.

