Yes, you can use natural language processing libraries like "natural" or "compromise" to identify the subject and verb of a sentence in JavaScript. Here's an example using the "natural" library:
```javascript
const natural = require('natural');
const sentence = "The quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog";
// Tokenize the sentence into individual words
const tokenizer = new natural.WordTokenizer();
const words = tokenizer.tokenize(sentence);
// Use the part-of-speech tagger to identify the verb and noun
const pos = new natural.BrillPOSTagger();
pos.load('english', () => {
  const taggedWords = pos.tag(words);
  let verb = '';
  let subject = '';
  taggedWords.forEach((taggedWord) => {
    const tag = taggedWord[1];
    const word = taggedWord[0];
    if (tag.startsWith('VB')) { // VB stands for verb
      verb = word;
    } else if (tag.startsWith('NN')) { // NN stands for noun
      subject = word;
    }
  });
  console.log(`Subject: ${subject}, Verb: ${verb}`);
});
```
This code tokenizes the sentence into individual words, then uses the Brill part-of-speech tagger to identify the verb and noun. The `startsWith` method is used to match the tags that start with "VB" or "NN". Finally, the subject and verb are printed to the console.

